ity cite101†email .\nL218: \nL219: * * *\nL220: # MORPH: Enable AI Accelerator for Zero Knowledge Proof\nL221: # 1. What is MORPH?\nL222: \nL223: MORPH is the first project to enable AI Accelerator, such as Google TPUs, to accelerate Zero Knowledge Proof Primitives (Multi-scalar Multiplication and Number Theory Transformation) and achieves the State-of-the-art (SotA) throughput and energy efficiency (performance per watt). Together with cite102†CROSS , they enable AI ASICs to be SotA throughput machine for cryptography primitive with wide-range precision.\nL224: \nL225: It features\nL226:   * MXU Lazy Modular Reduction: bringing quadratic high-precision modular reduction down to linear operation.\nL227: \nL228:   * dataflow optimization for MSM and NTT. Details in the cite99†paper†arxiv.org .\nL229: \nL230: This branch (`asplos`) contains demo scripts for profiling and comparing the two core workloads.\nL231: ## Project Structure\nL232: \nL233:     `├── finite_field_context.py           # Finite field arithmetic (MORPH & CROSS backends)\nL234:     ├── elliptic_curve_context.py         # Elliptic curve point arithmetic\nL235:     ├── multiscalar_multiplication_context.py    # Multi-scalar multiplication (MSM)\nL236:     ├── number_theory_transform_context.py       # Number Theoretic Transform (NTT)\nL237:     ├── utils.py                          # JAX kernel utilities, number theory helpers\nL238:     ├── profiler.py                       # Trace parsing and kernel profiling\nL239:     ├── configurations.toml               # Curve parameters (BLS12-377)\nL240:     ├── c_kernels/                        # Custom C kernels for TPU acceleration\nL241:     ├── deployments/                      # Serialized compiled JAX kernels\nL242:     `\nL243: All functions have `_test.py` and `_perf_test.py` for correctness and performance testing.\nL244: ## Key Concepts\nL245: Concept  | Description\nL246: --- | ---\nL247: DRNS (Double RNS)  | Residue Number System representation enabling efficient large-integer modular arithmetic on TPU\nL248: MORPH  | Alternative modular multiplication backend using chunk-based representation\nL249: MSM  | Multi-scalar multiplication — computing $\\sum_i s_i \\cdot P_i$ over elliptic curve points\nL250: Bucket Accumulation  | MSM decomposition strategy: scalars are sliced into windows, points accumulated into buckets per window\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/usenixsecurity26-lu-tao.pdf)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 20; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"id\":6}); Total lines: 1992\n"
